Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A modest, well-loved NURSERY. Toys are scattered everywhere.

A VELVETEEN RABBIT, fat and bunchy, with spotted brown and white fur, thread whiskers, and pink sateen ears, sits wedged in the top of a BOY'S stocking. A sprig of holly is between his paws.

The BOY, about six, tears open presents with gusto. He pulls the Rabbit from his stocking, hugging him tightly for a moment.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid. For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Aunts and Uncles arrive, filling the room with rustling paper and excited chatter. The Boy is distracted by new, shinier toys. The Velveteen Rabbit is set aside, forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - LATER

The Velveteen Rabbit sits in a toy cupboard, then on the nursery floor. Other toys, mechanical and expensive, look down on him. A MODEL BOAT, paint peeling, boasts of his "rigging." TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden LION, puts on airs.

The Rabbit feels insignificant. The only one kind to him is an old, bald-patched SKIN HORSE, whose brown coat shows seams underneath.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse smiles, a gentle, knowing expression.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs, looking at his own perfect velveteen fur.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA, the nursery maid, tidies the room with a swift, no-nonsense efficiency. Toys are swept into cupboards.

The Boy is getting ready for bed. He searches for his china dog.

NANA
(Impatient)
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

Nana grabs the Velveteen Rabbit by one ear and shoves him into the Boy's arms.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Velveteen Rabbit lies in the Boy's bed. The Boy hugs him tight, sometimes rolling over, sometimes pushing him under the pillow. The Rabbit misses the quiet talks with the Skin Horse.

But soon, he grows to like it. The Boy whispers secrets to him, makes tunnels under the bedclothes, and clasps him close all night.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy—so happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier, and his tail becoming unsewn, and all the pink rubbed off his nose where the Boy had kissed him.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring. The Boy and the Velveteen Rabbit spend long days in the garden. The Rabbit rides in a wheelbarrow, has picnics, and rests in fairy huts built under raspberry canes.

One evening, the Boy is called away. The Rabbit is left on the lawn until after dusk. Nana finds him, wet with dew and earthy from burrows. She grumbles as she rubs him with her apron.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Boy sits up in bed, reaching for the Rabbit.

NANA
(Scoffs)
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Velveteen Rabbit's boot-button eyes, long ago losing their polish, now hold a look of wisdom and beauty.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last. The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was a toy no longer. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - MORNING

Nana picks up the Velveteen Rabbit.

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOODS - SUMMER DAY

A wonderful summer. The Boy takes the Velveteen Rabbit to the woods after tea. He makes a cozy nest for the Rabbit among the bracken before wandering off to play.

The Rabbit lies alone, watching ants. Two WILD RABBITS creep out of the bracken. They are furry and brand-new, their seams invisible. They change shape as they move, long and thin, then fat and bunchy. Their feet pad softly. They twitch their noses.

The Velveteen Rabbit stares, looking for their clockwork. He sees none.

WILD RABBIT #1
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT #1
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

The Wild Rabbit hops sideways, standing on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT #1
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT #1
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit freezes. His back is all one piece, like a pincushion. He sits still, hoping they won't notice.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

The Wild Rabbit stretches his neck, peering closely.

WILD RABBIT #1
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

The Wild Rabbit laughs, then begins to whirl and dance.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT #1
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

The Velveteen Rabbit feels a funny, tickly feeling. He longs to dance.

The Wild Rabbit stops, comes close. His whiskers brush the Velveteen Rabbit's ear. He wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, and jumps back.

WILD RABBIT #1
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

The Rabbit's eyes well up. Footsteps approach. The Boy runs past. The two Wild Rabbits disappear with a stamp of feet and a flash of white tails.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

No answer. Only ants and swaying bracken. The Velveteen Rabbit is alone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a long time he lay very still, watching the bracken, and hoping that they would come back. But they never returned, and presently the sun sank lower and the little white moths fluttered out, and the Boy came and carried him home.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - DAY (WEEKS LATER)

Weeks pass. The Velveteen Rabbit is very old and shabby. His whiskers are gone, his ear lining grey, his spots faded. He's lost his shape.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
To the Boy he was always beautiful, and that was all that the little Rabbit cared about. He didn't mind how he looked to other people, because the nursery magic had made him Real, and when you are Real shabbiness doesn't matter.

The Boy lies in bed, face flushed, talking in his sleep. His body is hot. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night. The Velveteen Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes, afraid to stir.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a long weary time, for the Boy was too ill to play, and the little Rabbit found it rather dull with nothing to do all day long. But he snuggled down patiently, and looked forward to the time when the Boy should be well again.

The Boy slowly recovers. He sits up, looking at picture-books with the Rabbit cuddled close. One day, he gets up and dresses.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - MORNING

A bright, sunny morning. Windows wide open. The Boy is on the balcony, wrapped in a shawl. The Velveteen Rabbit lies tangled in the bedclothes.

He overhears Nana and a DOCTOR talking.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Rabbit's heart sinks.

EXT. GARDEN - BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

The Velveteen Rabbit is in a sack with old picture-books and rubbish, behind the fowl-house. The sack is untied. He wriggles his head out.

He shivers. His coat is thin, threadbare. He sees the raspberry canes where he played with the Boy. A great sadness comes over him. He thinks of the Skin Horse, of becoming Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
(V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A single, real tear trickles down his shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

Where the tear falls, a mysterious flower grows. Slender green leaves, a blossom like a golden cup. The Rabbit watches, mesmerized. The blossom opens.

A FAIRY steps out. Her dress is pearl and dew-drops, flowers in her hair. She gathers the Rabbit in her arms, kisses his damp nose.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

The Rabbit stares, a flicker of recognition.

FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

The Fairy holds the Rabbit close and flies into the wood.

EXT. WOODS - NIGHT

The moon has risen. The forest is beautiful, bracken fronds like frosted silver. Wild rabbits dance in a glade. They stop, forming a ring, staring at the Fairy.

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the Velveteen Rabbit again and puts him down on the grass.

FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit sits still, remembering his lack of hind legs. He doesn't know the Fairy's last kiss changed him.

Something tickles his nose. Instinctively, he lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

He has hind legs! Instead of velveteen, he has soft, shiny brown fur. His ears twitch. His whiskers are long.

He gives a leap, springing about the turf, jumping sideways, whirling round like the other rabbits. He is so excited that when he finally stops to look for the Fairy, she is gone.

He is a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOODS - SPRING DAY (A YEAR LATER)

Autumn and Winter pass. Spring returns. The Boy plays in the wood behind the house.

Two rabbits creep out from the bracken, peeping at him. One is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted.

The Boy looks at the second rabbit. About his soft nose and round black eyes, there is something familiar.

BOY
(To himself)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

FADE OUT.
